# @@GARDEN@@ — journal

Append-only. Never rewritten, never summarised: a summary is a second copy that can disagree with the
first, and this file merges by union. Every state-change to the garden is logged here in the same commit
that makes it — the gate REFUSES a staged bean whose change is not recorded, and a change to the
vocabulary or the law must say RULE-CHANGE distinctly.

One entry per change, newest last. Its heading is written by `bin/dmjournal.py`, which reads the moment from
the clock — never typed: the gate refuses a heading that tool did not write. Give it who you are, one line
saying what changed, and the body; it writes the heading above the body and appends both:

    python3 bin/dmjournal.py "<human (name) | agent>" "<one line: what changed>" --body "- action: what was done"

(`python` on Windows. A line break inside the quotes is kept, in a Unix shell and in PowerShell alike.)
An entry reads, once written:

    ## <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM+HH:MM> · <human (name) | agent> · <one line: what changed>
    - action: what was done
    - detail: what a cold reader needs in order to check it
    - why: the reason, which is the part that does not survive anywhere else
    - refs: the files touched
